### 2. Fair probe comparison: the probe on its most confident passages

The verbalizer may stay silent; the probe may not. So the probe is scored only on its most confident N% of the same 540 passages, N being the share of explanations in which the verbalizer names anything of the right type. Confidence = the probe's top predicted probability.

| tokens after entity | N (verbalizer names something) | probe, all 540 passages | probe, most confident N% | verbalizer precision when it names |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 100.0% | 1.000 [1.000, 1.000] | 1.000 [1.000, 1.000] (n=540) | 1.000 [0.999, 1.000] (n=2160) |
| 1 | 79.6% | 0.622 [0.574, 0.670] | 0.688 [0.639, 0.739] (n=430) | 0.675 [0.624, 0.723] (n=1720) |
| 4 | 47.2% | 0.431 [0.374, 0.489] | 0.522 [0.429, 0.619] (n=255) | 0.460 [0.396, 0.524] (n=1019) |
| 16 | 29.3% | 0.354 [0.294, 0.415] | 0.513 [0.417, 0.611] (n=158) | 0.254 [0.188, 0.325] (n=633) |

### 3. What the wrong names are, at 4 and 16 tokens after the entity

Every wrong name in every explanation at that distance, split by whether it is one of the 9 other entities we listed for the type or a same-type entity outside our list. By construction none occurs in the passage.

| tokens after entity | wrong names in total | one of our listed entities | same-type entity outside our list | most common |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | 1255 | 261 (21%) | 994 (79%) | guitar (27), dog (25), piano (21), lion (20), drum (17), Canada (15), wolf (14), bird (13), London (11), Nehru (10), horse (10), United States (9) |
| 16 | 825 | 128 (16%) | 697 (84%) | Toronto (12), piano (12), guitar (11), Italy (10), America (9), serpent (8), eggs (8), mammal (7), dog (7), food product (7), Turkey (6), Britain (6) |

